-## Subtopics
-
-### Point Plane Test Traversal
-Uses point-plane tests for ray-tetrahedron intersection. Chosen when precise intersection is needed without BVH overhead. [Point Plane Test Traversal](982-point-plane-test-traversal.md)
-
-### Coarse Mesh Traversal with Dual-Ray Culling
-Uses dual-ray culling to skip empty regions. Chosen when empty space culling is more effective than BVH traversal. [Coarse Mesh Traversal with Dual-Ray Culling](987-coarse-mesh-traversal-with-dual-ray-culling.md)
-
-### Two-level Nested Ray Marching
-Uses a two-level marching approach. Chosen for uniform grids or simple hierarchical structures. [Two-level Nested Ray Marching](1045-two-level-nested-ray-marching.md)
-
-### Hierarchical Digital Differential Analyzer (HDDA)
-Uses HDDA for traversal. Chosen for specific hardware acceleration or fixed-function pipelines. [Hierarchical Digital Differential Analyzer (HDDA)](1122-hierarchical-digital-differential-analyzer-hdda.md)
-
-### Octree-based Volume Rendering
-Uses octrees for spatial partitioning. Chosen for voxel-based data. [Octree-based Volume Rendering](1131-octree-based-volume-rendering.md)
-
-### Multi-Resolution Rendering with K-D Tree
-Uses K-D trees for multi-resolution data. Chosen for general-purpose hierarchical acceleration. [Multi-Resolution Rendering with K-D Tree](1942-multi-resolution-rendering-with-k-d-tree.md)
-
-### Parametric Octree Traversal
-Uses parametric representations in octrees. Chosen for smooth, parametric volumes. [Parametric Octree Traversal](2361-parametric-octree-traversal.md)
-
-### CPU Sparse Voxel Octree Traversal
-Optimized for CPU execution with sparse voxels. Chosen for CPU-based rendering of sparse data. [CPU Sparse Voxel Octree Traversal](2455-cpu-sparse-voxel-octree-traversal.md)
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 **Time and Memory Complexity:**
-*   The number of elements in the coarse mesh (and thus the BVH) grows exponentially with the refinement level $k$. For the Uniform Engine dataset, the number of elements increases from 6 at level 0 to 196,608 at level 5 [S1]. For the Engine dataset, it increases from 5,177 at level 0 to 14,402,140 at level 5 [S1].
+*   The number of elements in the coarse mesh (and thus the BVH) grows exponentially with the refinement level $k$ [S1]. For the Uniform Engine dataset, the number of elements increases from 6 at level 0 to 196,608 at level 5 [S1]. For the Engine dataset, it increases from 5,177 at level 0 to 14,402,140 at level 5 [S1].
 *   BVH construction and traversal time increase with the number of elements, but the efficiency of the BVH decreases as the number of elements increases [S1].
 
